NIL lock packet regression (host only)
=====================================

From this directory:

    cc -std=c99 -Wall -Wextra -Werror -fno-strict-aliasing \
       -fsanitize=address,undefined \
       -DAROS_FAST_BPTR nil_host.c -o /tmp/nil_host_fast
    /tmp/nil_host_fast
    cc -std=c99 -Wall -Wextra -Werror -fno-strict-aliasing \
       -fsanitize=address,undefined \
       nil_host.c -o /tmp/nil_host_shifted
    /tmp/nil_host_shifted

The test includes the production rom/dos/nil.c with packet-boundary types
and allocation/release spies. It supplies the same FH_FROM_LOCK arguments
as OpenFromLock: argument 1 is the destination handle; argument 2 is the
source lock. It checks that the destination is initialized, the source
lock is consumed exactly once at its decoded address, and its bytes are
not overwritten as though it were a FileHandle. A separate case checks
the FREE_LOCK BPTR-to-address conversion.

The original FH_FROM_LOCK code fails both builds. The original FREE_LOCK
code passes the fast build and fails the shifted build. Both cases pass
after the correction. No global memory-availability measurement or task
timing is used; the release spy records an incorrect pointer without
dereferencing or freeing it.

This models both BPTR conversions on the host, not two native target ABIs.
It covers successful allocation and packet ownership only. It does not
test allocation failure, the FREE_LOCK return convention, other NIL
actions, FAT/other filesystem handlers, or an entire Shell launch. The
target dos/nil test remains a separate API integration test.

The negative control deliberately executes the old handler's wrong-type
store into the source lock. Compile this harness with -fno-strict-aliasing
so that observation does not depend on type-based alias optimization.
Sanitizer-clean execution does not validate that historical store as
portable C, nor establish an optimized native ABI result.
